Q: How to filter Java Streams using Files.getAttribute A: <p>You cannot compare a &quot;creationTime&quot; / <code>FileTime</code>  with &quot;&gt;&quot;. Instead convert <code>toInstant</code> and use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/time/Instant.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Instant</a> <code>isBefore</code> or <code>isAfter</code>.</p>
<p>If you view source of <code>Files.isDirectory(path)</code> and <code>Files.getAttribute(path,attr)</code> you will see that both filters each read the underlying filesystem attributes - accessing the filesystem for every check made.</p>
<p>Switching to <code>Files.find</code> provides a more efficient way to scan as it retrieves file attributes once automatically, and passes both the filesystem path and attributes to a bi-predicate to make the checks.</p>
<p>This makes it easier to pre-filter a <code>Files.find</code> stream by directory and then by comparing the creation times using built-in <code>time1.isAfter(time2)</code>. Something like this:</p>
<pre><code>Instant refTime = Files.readAttributes(reference, BasicFileAttributes.class).creationTime().toInstant();
try(var dirs = Files.find(dir,
         1 /* depth use up to Integer.MAX_VALUE */,
         (p,a) -&gt; a.isDirectory()
               &amp;&amp; a.creationTime().toInstant().isAfter(refTime))) {
    List&lt;Path&gt; recentDirs = dirs.toList();
    ...
}
</code></pre>

Q: Storage duration of compound literals in C A: <blockquote>
<p>Storage duration of compound literals in C</p>
</blockquote>
<p>With the below code, <em>compound literal</em> <code>(int []){a, b}</code> exists from its creation to the closing <code>}</code>.  In this case that is also where the function ends.  The <em>address</em> to that compound literal (saved in <code>foo-&gt;i</code>) ceases to be valid then too.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>void f(struct s *foo, const int a, const int b)
{
    foo-&gt;i = (int []){a, b};
} // &lt;-- Valid until here.
</code></pre>
<p>After that point, dereferencing that address is <em>undefined behavior</em> (UB), as in the calling code <code>foo.i[0]</code>.</p>
<p>Technically, even <em>reading</em> that address is also <em>undefined behavior</em> (UB), yet I have never come across a  system that did not tolerate the read of an invalid object address.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Since the code above runs without error, does it mean that the compilers created the array (int []){34, 35} in the global scope, therefore it has static storage duration?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;code above runs without error&quot; is an incorrect conclusion.  OP's observed results are reliant on UB, which may differ in the future.</p>
<p><code>(int []){34, 35}</code> in the global scope --&gt; no.<br />
... therefore it has static storage --&gt; duration? --&gt; no.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Should I stop using clang lol (jk)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No.</p>
<hr />
<p>Alternatives include:</p>
<p><strong>Allocate</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// foo-&gt;i = (int []){a, b};

#define CL (int []){a, b}
foo-&gt;i = malloc(sizeof CL);
if (foo-&gt;i == NULL) {
  Handle_OOM_with TBD_code();
} else {
  memcpy(foo-&gt;i, &amp;CL, sizeof CL);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Make static</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>void f(struct s *foo, const int a, const int b)
{
    static int ar[2];
    ar[0] = a;
    ar[1] = b;
    foo-&gt;i = &amp;ar;
}
</code></pre>
